From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Xiaoxing Hu <hi@xiaoxing.dev>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 20:33:00 +1300
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] embedded: a Linux platform whose host presents the
 renderer's frames

GHOSTTY_PLATFORM_LINUX is a surface with no view to draw into: the OpenGL
renderer exports each finished frame, as the GTK app has it do, and the
host takes it with ghostty_surface_take_frame and shows it, on a Wayland
subsurface for one. ghostty_surface_set_presentation_health asks for
frames in memory when the host cannot show DMABUFs.

A host that takes frames on a thread of its own gives a callback with the
surface. It runs on the render thread for each frame, in place of the
redraw notice through the app's mailbox: that notice is delivered by the
app's tick, and one a frame fills the mailbox while the host's main thread
is busy, after which the render thread waits on it.

The texture a frame is exported from is no longer drawn into while the
frame is out. The swap chain takes its next target as soon as a frame is
queued, and a presenter still reads the last one then, so a slow presenter
showed frames torn between two. The export textures leave the render
targets for a pool of their own, opengl/ExportPool.zig: a frame holds its
texture until it is freed, a held texture is never copied into, the pool
grows to six and past that a frame is dropped and drawn again once a
texture is free. A frame names its texture with an id, so that a host
keeps one imported buffer a texture. Freeing a frame only clears a flag,
from any thread and also after the surface is gone; GL objects are deleted
on the render thread. The GTK app, which frees a frame's planes when GDK
drops the texture built from them, would hold a texture until then: that is
read from its source, and it was not built with this change.

ghostty_surface_draw drew on the caller's thread, where OpenGL has no
context: with OpenGL it now asks the render thread for a frame, drawn even
if nothing changed.
